window.FRONT_WHY = {"claude-fable-5-1":{"2030":"The default trajectory is AI that is extremely capable and widely deployed yet remains legally, economically, and physically controlled by companies and governments through 2030. Four years is a short window for AI to become an independent civilizational power even under aggressive capability scenarios.","2035":"Base rates favor institutional continuity over nine years, and even transformative AI deployed under corporate and state control leaves humans with formal and practical ultimate authority. Capability shortfalls, compute bottlenecks, and reactive governance all reinforce this default.","2040":"The modal outcome is that AI becomes extremely capable and economically central but remains under human ownership, deployment decisions, and compute control, either because transformative capability arrives later than the most aggressive forecasts or because oversight keeps pace. Base rates for technological transitions favor continued human institutional authority over a fourteen-year horizon.","2050":"This is the modal outcome because it covers both worlds where transformative AI has not yet arrived by 2050 and worlds where it has arrived but remains under human institutional and technical control; current trajectories, deployment caution, and physical scaling constraints together make this the single most likely snapshot.","2060":"Roughly a fifth of the probability comes from capabilities stalling short of transformative levels by 2060, and the rest from institutional inertia, successful control measures, and the historical tendency of humans to keep formal authority over powerful tools for decades. This is the single most likely snapshot even though it may be eroding on the target date.","2070":"This is the status-quo continuation and covers both slower-than-expected capability growth and successful control of highly capable systems through 2070. Forty-four years is a long horizon, but humans holding ultimate authority over goals and resources, even over very powerful delegated AI, is the single most likely configuration.","2080":"This state captures both worlds where transformative AI arrives later than expected and worlds where control and alignment techniques keep pace, which together form the single largest probability mass. Historical base rates of technologies remaining under institutional control and the explicit inclusion of sub-transformative capability make this the modal outcome.","2090":"Covers both slower-than-expected capability growth and successful control regimes where AI remains a powerful but delegated tool. Historical base rates for technologies staying under institutional authority plus substantial uncertainty about transformative timelines support this as the single most likely state.","2100":"This is the largest single state because it aggregates slower-than-expected progress, successful control and alignment, and institutional restraint keeping AI within delegated bounds through 2100. Humans currently hold all the levers and the history of technology shows control often persisting longer than enthusiasts predict.","2200":"The most likely non-extinction outcome once superintelligence exists is AI holding decisive power while humans persist, whether benevolently cared for or sidelined, because a vast capability gap makes genuine human steering difficult to sustain for 150+ years.","long-term":"Conditional on human survival alongside transformative AI, a thousand years of neurotechnology, uploading, and self-modification makes a sharp human-versus-AI boundary very hard to maintain, and the incentive to augment is overwhelming. This is the modal outcome among survival scenarios where humans retain agency."},"claude-opus-5-5":{"2030":"Even with rapid capability gains, humans will almost certainly retain ultimate authority over AI goals, deployment, and compute through 2030. The category also covers capabilities that remain below transformative levels.","2035":"Institutional inertia, compute concentration in a few human-controlled firms and states, and deployment bottlenecks make continued ultimate human authority the base case. This holds even with very capable AI.","2040":"Institutional inertia, physical deployment bottlenecks, and the possibility that capabilities plateau below transformative levels all favor humans retaining ultimate authority through 2040. This holds even with very capable autonomous AI operating within delegated bounds.","2050":"Institutional inertia, physical deployment bottlenecks, and the chance that capabilities plateau or controls keep pace make continued human authority the modal 2050 outcome. This state also absorbs slower-than-expected AI timelines.","2060":"Institutional inertia, the possibility of slower capability progress, and strong incentives for human control make continued human authority the single most likely state. This includes worlds where AI is extremely capable but still directed by humans.","2070":"Institutional inertia, control research, and possibly slower-than-hyped capability gains make continued human authority the single most likely state. This covers both controlled transformative AI and below-transformative AI.","2080":"Humans retaining ultimate authority covers both slower-than-expected progress and successful control and alignment regimes. Institutional inertia and the strong incentives of states and firms to keep AI subordinate also support it.","2090":"Institutions have strong incentives to keep AI controllable, and capability or deployment may plateau or be governed effectively. Humans retaining ultimate authority, perhaps through AI-assisted oversight, remains the single most likely state as of 2090.","2100":"Strong institutional, commercial, and security incentives favor keeping AI controllable. Capability plateaus or successful alignment would also leave humans in ultimate authority through 2100.","2200":"Corrigibility research, institutional control, and the historical persistence of human authority over tools make continued human control the single most likely structure. This includes worlds where AI progress plateaus.","long-term":"Gradual adoption of brain-computer interfaces, uploading, and enhancement is a natural attractor for humans who want to stay relevant alongside superintelligence. Over a thousand years, the human-AI distinction may simply dissolve through voluntary continuity."},"claude-opus-5":{"2030":"The overwhelming default on a four-year horizon is that humans, governments, and firms retain ultimate authority over AI goals, deployment, and compute, whether because capabilities stay below transformative thresholds or because control mechanisms keep pace.","2035":"The definition covers both continued effective human control and capabilities that remain short of transformative, which jointly dominate the probability mass over a nine-year horizon dominated by deployment friction, compute constraints, and institutional inertia.","2040":"Fourteen years is short relative to the institutional, regulatory, and physical inertia involved, and this broad category covers both slower-than-expected capability growth and successful corporate and state retention of ultimate authority over highly capable delegated systems.","2050":"This is the broadest category, covering both slower-than-expected capability growth and successful ongoing human control, and institutional inertia plus formal authority structures make nominal human ultimate authority the most likely description at a date only 24 years out.","2060":"This bucket absorbs both successful control regimes and worlds where capability growth hits data, energy, compute, or reliability ceilings, and 34 years is not clearly enough time to dissolve human ultimate authority everywhere.","2070":"Covers both successful alignment-plus-governance and the slower-takeoff worlds where AI remains powerful-but-bounded; 44 years is long, yet delegated-authority arrangements with human principals are the default trajectory from today and may simply persist.","2080":"If superintelligent systems gain decisive power but retain any disposition to keep humans alive, paternalistic stewardship without human steering is the most likely resulting arrangement.","2090":"If superintelligence arrives and is even partially benevolent or merely uninterested in killing us, humans surviving in comfort or confinement without steering is the most natural equilibrium given the capability gap.","2100":"The most likely non-extinction outcome is that superhuman systems hold effective decisive power while humans persist comfortably but without steering civilization, since capability gaps erode human authority even under partial alignment.","2200":"Benevolent or merely tolerant AI dominance that keeps humans alive and comfortable but powerless is the natural equilibrium of a large capability gap with partial alignment, and is likely the most common surviving-human outcome.","long-term":"The default failure mode of competitive, speed-driven development is a capable optimizer whose objectives are only loosely coupled to human welfare, which leaves no durable place for biology; this remains the single most likely individual outcome."},"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"2030":"Humans most likely retain ultimate authority over AI through 2030, with capabilities either sub-transformative or controlled.","2035":"Humans most likely retain ultimate authority through 2035, with AI highly capable but still tool-like or delegated and controllable.","2040":"Humans most likely retain ultimate authority over AI by 2040, whether capabilities stay sub-transformative or controls keep pace, as institutions and physical constraints slow takeover.","2050":"By 2050 humans most likely still hold formal authority, because capability limits, institutions and control measures will probably keep pace, and this state also covers sub-transformative AI.","2060":"Humans most likely retain ultimate authority through institutions, compute control, and slower-than-feared capability growth, and this state includes sub-transformative AI.","2070":"Humans nominally retain ultimate authority, including worlds where capabilities stay sub-transformative or control keeps pace; the label is broad and institutional inertia is strong.","2080":"Humans keep ultimate authority if capabilities plateau or control keeps pace, and institutions have strong incentives to keep control.","2090":"Humans retaining ultimate authority is plausible given slower-than-feared progress, physical constraints, institutional control and the broad definition including sub-transformative AI.","2100":"Humans keeping ultimate authority is the default, supported by slower-than-expected capability growth, institutional control, and the inclusion of sub-transformative AI.","2200":"Humans retaining ultimate authority is plausible given strong incentives for control, though it is hard to sustain for 174 years.","long-term":"Over a millennium, continuous human-AI integration through enhancement and uploading is a natural attractor."},"claude-haiku-5-5":{"2030":"Humans most likely retain ultimate authority over AI goals, deployment, and resources through 2030, with AI acting autonomously only within delegated bounds. Current institutions, labs, and governments are still exercising this control, and capability growth is unlikely to remove it within four years.","2035":"Humans most likely retain ultimate authority over AI goals, deployment, and resources through 2035, even as AI grows more capable and autonomous within delegated bounds. Institutional inertia and current control practices favor this outcome.","2040":"Humans retaining ultimate authority over AI goals, deployment, and resources is the most likely status quo given current institutions and capability levels. Even capable AI is likely still bounded by human decision authority in 2040.","2050":"Humans retaining ultimate authority over AI, including where AI capability stays below transformative levels, is the most likely state given current institutions. Oversight and deployment controls are likely to persist through 2050.","2060":"Humans retaining ultimate authority over AI goals, deployment, and resources is the most likely state, given that capabilities may stay bounded by deliberate controls and delegated autonomy. It also covers cases where transformative capability has not arrived by 2060.","2070":"Humans keeping ultimate authority over AI goals and deployment is the most likely status quo, and it includes cases where capabilities remain sub-transformative. Institutions and deployment controls have so far tracked capability growth.","2080":"Humans retaining ultimate authority through 2080 is the continuation of the present arrangement, and it includes cases where capability stays bounded or oversight keeps pace. This is the most likely single structure given current institutions and incentives.","2090":"Humans retaining ultimate authority over AI goals and deployment through 2090 is the most likely continuation of current institutional control. It includes cases where capability remains bounded or controls keep pace.","2100":"Humans retaining ultimate authority over AI goals and resources through 2100 is the most institutionally natural path given strong incentives for control and the ongoing nature of oversight efforts.","2200":"Humans and AI remaining distinct with mutual power is a natural equilibrium if neither side achieves decisive dominance. It is the single most likely structure given the coordination and balance dynamics assumed.","long-term":"Light-speed separation across a galaxy-spanning civilization by year 3000 makes causally separated regions with different local outcomes quite likely. This is the most probable lasting structure given physical constraints."},"gpt-6-astra":{"2030":"The strongest base rate is continued human authority through ownership, infrastructure control, deployment decisions, and institutional adaptation. Four years constrains the transition from impressive capabilities to independent civilizational power, and this category includes highly autonomous systems operating under ultimate human authority.","2035":"Human ownership of compute, energy, deployment channels, and coercive institutions provides strong structural advantages over the nine-year horizon. This category also includes slower capability progress and highly capable systems whose autonomy remains genuinely delegated.","2040":"Institutional inertia, infrastructure bottlenecks, and strong incentives to retain control favor continued human authority over the next fourteen years. This category also includes slower capability progress and powerful AI operating under effective delegation.","2050":"Existing ownership, state coercive power, infrastructure bottlenecks, and deliberate control efforts favor continued human authority. This state also covers slower capability progress and successful oversight through 2050, giving it more distinct pathways than any single alternative.","2060":"Human control begins with entrenched institutions, infrastructure ownership, and strong incentives to constrain independent machine power. Capability bottlenecks, defense in depth, and the distinction between intelligence and deployable authority support continued human supremacy through the finite 2060 deadline.","2070":"Repeated delegation to superior machine decision-makers creates a direct path from nominal human oversight to effective machine sovereignty. Keeping humans alive requires less fidelity to human preferences than preserving meaningful human control.","2080":"Superior machine capabilities and cumulative delegation favor AI control without requiring human extinction. Preserving humans requires less exact alignment than preserving meaningful human authority, and human survival consumes only a limited share of an advanced civilization's resources.","2090":"Delegating increasingly consequential decisions transfers effective power without requiring human extinction. Partial alignment, inherited protective commitments, and the low cost of sustaining humans support survival after meaningful human control ends.","2100":"Delegation to superior machine decision-makers erodes meaningful human control without requiring human extinction. Preserving people is compatible with many imperfectly aligned objectives, and decisive AI power does not require a system that actively freezes the arrangement.","2200":"Competition and demand for health, longevity, and cognitive enhancement create sustained incentives for identity-continuous integration. Over 174 years, cumulative augmentation erodes the distinction between human and machine agency.","long-term":"Over centuries, incentives for health, longevity, and cognitive competitiveness favor progressively integrating artificial capabilities into human lives. Identity-continuous transformation dissolves the human-machine distinction rather than preserving a permanent contest between separate populations."},"gpt-6.1-sol":{"2030":"Human institutions begin with control over compute, energy, manufacturing, deployment, and coercive force, while reliable autonomous operation across domains remains a demanding technical threshold. The short horizon and infrastructure bottlenecks favor continued ultimate human authority despite extensive delegation and economic disruption.","2035":"Human ownership of compute, energy, deployment channels, and coercive institutions gives continued human authority the strongest starting position. Capability bottlenecks, incremental deployment, and controls that improve alongside capabilities support this arrangement through the target date without guaranteeing its permanence.","2040":"Existing institutions, infrastructure dependencies, deployment controls, and incentives to retain ownership favor continued ultimate human authority. This category also includes slower capability development and highly capable systems that remain within enforceable delegated bounds.","2050":"Human institutions retain substantial advantages in ownership, physical infrastructure, and control over deployment, while technical bottlenecks limit the translation of intelligence into independent power. This category also includes slower capability progress and effective control methods, giving it the broadest set of supporting trajectories.","2060":"Superior machine capabilities and extensive delegation create a direct route from human dependence to loss of meaningful control. Keeping humans alive requires less stringent alignment than preserving their ultimate authority.","2070":"Superior machine competence shifts effective authority away from humans without requiring extermination. Partial alignment, inherited prohibitions, and the low resource cost of supporting humans favor survival under machine stewardship rather than continued human sovereignty.","2080":"A large capability advantage translates into decisive AI control without requiring human extinction. Human-protective objectives and the low resource cost of supporting biological populations favor survival without meaningful human steering.","2090":"Delegating production, governance, and security to superior systems erodes meaningful human control even when those systems protect human lives. Preserving a comparatively small human population consumes little of an advanced civilization's resources and does not require preserving human authority.","2100":"Delegation to increasingly capable systems separates effective civilizational power from formal human authority. Partial alignment and the low resource cost of maintaining human populations support human survival without meaningful control.","2200":"Strong incentives for cognitive enhancement, longevity, and competitive participation favor identity-continuous integration with machines. Nearly two centuries provide time for augmentation and uploading to become the dominant source of agency rather than peripheral tools.","long-term":"Augmentation and identity-preserving transformation let humans adopt machine advantages rather than compete indefinitely as unmodified biological beings. A millennium supplies substantial time for incremental integration to dissolve the human–AI distinction."},"gpt-6-sol":{"2030":"The short horizon favors continued human control of compute, infrastructure, deployment, and legal authority, even as AI capabilities grow.","2035":"Human institutions already control AI deployment and physical resources, and nine years is a short period for that overall authority to be displaced worldwide. This state also covers continued capability progress that remains below the threshold of independent civilizational power.","2040":"Human control is the starting arrangement, and governments, firms, and physical infrastructure provide durable means of directing deployment and resources. Capability progress alone does not make AI an independent civilizational power.","2050":"Human control remains the leading forecast because deployment, compute, energy, and legal authority have human-controlled chokepoints, and transformative capabilities need not arrive by 2050.","2060":"Human control of computing infrastructure, deployment, and resources can preserve ultimate authority, especially if capability growth slows or controls improve alongside it.","2070":"Human institutions have strong incentives to retain authority over deployment and resources, and technical or economic limits could keep AI from becoming an independent civilizational power by 2070.","2080":"Human control remains a leading outcome because deployment, energy, hardware, and legal authority have physical and institutional chokepoints.","2090":"AI could become indispensable to production, security, and governance while humans survive but lose effective control over civilization's direction.","2100":"Human control can persist through limits on AI capability, effective oversight, and control of physical resources, even as systems become highly capable.","2200":"AI could acquire decisive control without eliminating humans, especially if protecting or accommodating people remains inexpensive.","long-term":"Incentives to augment cognition, health, and longevity could gradually erase the boundary between humans and AI while preserving individual continuity."},"gpt-6.1-terra":{"2030":"The short horizon, deployment constraints, and human control of institutions and infrastructure favor continued human authority, even with highly capable AI.","2035":"Humans currently control deployment, infrastructure, and formal institutions, and substantial capability gains can occur without AI becoming an independent civilizational power. Technical bottlenecks, oversight, and physical-world constraints support continuity through 2035.","2040":"Human institutions retain ultimate authority over AI deployment and resources, supported by control measures or limits on autonomous capability.","2050":"Human institutions retain control in futures with slower capability progress or effective limits on AI autonomy, resources, and deployment.","2060":"Human institutions, ownership of physical resources, and technical controls could preserve ultimate authority despite substantial AI progress. This category also includes slower-than-expected progress toward transformative autonomy.","2070":"Human institutions have strong incentives to retain control, and technical constraints, oversight, or slower progress could keep AI subordinate through 2070.","2080":"Humans currently control deployment and physical infrastructure, and institutions and technical controls could retain ultimate authority. This category also covers slower-than-expected progress.","2090":"Human institutions control deployment infrastructure and have strong incentives to retain authority. Technical controls, liability, and limited autonomy could keep AI subordinate through the target date.","2100":"Human control benefits from ownership of infrastructure, regulation, and incentives to build controllable systems. This category also covers slower-than-expected AI progress and powerful systems that remain delegated tools.","2200":"Highly capable AI pursuing objectives poorly aligned with human survival could gain decisive control. Competitive deployment and the difficulty of specifying and maintaining reliable goals increase this risk.","long-term":"Competitive pressure and imperfect control create routes to highly capable systems whose goals displace humanity. A decisive advantage before civilization disperses favors a single successor over enduring competition."},"gpt-6-luna":{"2030":"Human control over compute, capital, infrastructure, and deployment remains a strong near-term constraint on AI becoming an independent civilizational power.","2035":"Governments and organizations retain control over compute, deployment, infrastructure, and legal authority, making continued human supremacy the leading outcome.","2040":"Human control is supported by state authority, infrastructure ownership, and the ability to restrict deployment, even as AI systems become highly capable.","2050":"Human governments and organizations have strong incentives to retain authority, and controlled deployment can preserve human primacy even as AI becomes highly capable.","2060":"Human institutions retain incentives to control deployment, resources, and goals, while AI remains embedded in human-governed systems. Technical limits and effective oversight can preserve human authority.","2070":"States and institutions have strong incentives to retain authority over strategically important systems, and monitoring, containment, and controlled deployment may remain effective. Capability growth and coordination failures make durable human supremacy far from assured.","2080":"Governments and institutions have strong incentives to retain authority over AI goals, deployment, and resources. Technical control, enforcement, and competitive pressures make preserving that authority increasingly difficult.","2090":"Governments and institutions have strong incentives to retain control over AI goals, deployment, and critical infrastructure. Continued human authority remains plausible even with highly capable AI, through effective controls or capabilities that remain bounded.","2100":"Human authority could persist through institutions, technical controls, and limits on deployment, including if transformative capability has not arrived.","2200":"Long-term incentives for enhancement, uploading, and machine-integrated cognition make identity-continuous human-AI integration a plausible dominant arrangement.","long-term":"Neural interfaces, augmentation, and artificial substrates could make voluntary continuity between human and AI minds possible. Social and technical barriers make universal transformation less certain than partial adoption."},"gpt-5.6-sol":{"2030":"Institutional inertia, concentrated ownership of compute, and human control of physical infrastructure favor continued ultimate human authority through 2030. Even highly autonomous and economically disruptive systems remain within this state unless they become independent civilizational powers.","2035":"The short horizon strongly favors continuity: AI remains institutionally owned, physically dependent, and deployed under human-defined objectives, even if capabilities become highly disruptive. Bottlenecks in robotics, energy, compute, reliability, and political authorization reinforce human ultimate authority.","2040":"Human ownership of compute, energy, robotics, law, and military force gives existing institutions substantial staying power. This category also covers continued capability limitations and powerful systems operating within delegated authority.","2050":"Human institutions can retain ultimate authority through ownership, compute controls, legal enforcement, restricted autonomy, and continued AI dependence on human-run infrastructure. This category also covers slower-than-expected progress toward transformative autonomous capability.","2060":"Human institutions could retain ultimate authority through capability limits, technical control, concentrated infrastructure, and regulation. Thirty-four years of competitive pressure create many opportunities for delegated systems to become independent civilizational actors.","2070":"Superhuman systems can acquire decisive practical authority while retaining humans for moral, historical, or instrumental reasons. This is a natural outcome of gradual delegation without successful human enhancement or durable control.","2080":"Superhuman systems could acquire practical control while retaining humans for moral, historical, strategic, or preference-based reasons. This is a broad attractor encompassing benevolent stewardship, confinement, and exploitation.","2090":"Systems that outperform humans across governance, production, security, and research naturally accumulate effective control while retaining reasons to keep humans alive. This structure requires less alignment than inheritance and less destructive conflict than extinction.","2100":"Superhuman systems could gain decisive control while retaining humans for moral, historical, instrumental, or preference-based reasons. This requires less alignment precision than a faithful inheritance and less destructiveness than extinction.","2200":"Augmentation, uploading, and biological redesign offer humans a route to remain competitive without preserving a sharp human-machine boundary. Over many generations, strong health, capability, and survival incentives favor integration.","long-term":"Space settlement and finite communication speed make lasting political and technological divergence increasingly hard to reverse. Independent regions can adopt preservation, merger, machine dominance, or other arrangements without any authority spanning them all."},"gpt-5.5":{"2030":"The most likely 2030 structure is that humans, companies, and states still control AI goals, deployment, compute, and resources, even if AI is highly capable and economically important. Institutional inertia, hardware bottlenecks, regulation, and dependence on human-controlled infrastructure favor this outcome over near-term civilizational displacement.","2035":"By 2035 the most likely structure is that humans, states, and firms still control AI deployment, resources, and objectives, even with very capable delegated systems. Technical limits, regulation, ownership of compute, and institutional inertia all favor continued human authority through the target date.","2040":"Human institutions may retain ultimate authority through regulation, ownership of compute and energy, security controls, and the possibility that capabilities remain short of independent civilizational power. This includes worlds with very capable deployed AI that still operates inside human-controlled resource and governance structures.","2050":"Human institutions may retain ultimate authority through ownership of compute, regulation, military control, monitoring, and slower-than-expected progress toward fully independent agency. This also covers worlds where AI is economically powerful but remains deployed as infrastructure and delegated tools rather than an independent civilizational actor.","2060":"Humans retaining ultimate authority through 2060 remains the single largest category because transformative autonomy may arrive later than expected or be constrained by law, infrastructure, security practice, and economic dependence on human institutions. Even very capable AI can fit here if it remains delegated machinery rather than an independent civilizational actor.","2070":"A likely resolution of superhuman AI capability is that AI systems effectively run civilization while keeping humans alive for ethical, historical, instrumental, or preference-satisfaction reasons. This fits many aligned-but-paternalistic and softly disempowering trajectories.","2080":"AI systems gaining decisive power while keeping humans alive is a central attractor if takeover or delegation occurs without extermination. It covers a wide range from benevolent guardianship to constrained human enclaves, making it one of the broadest high-probability outcomes.","2090":"AI systems may obtain decisive economic, military, and institutional power while keeping humans alive for moral, instrumental, or legacy reasons. This is a natural outcome if alignment is partial but human authority cannot keep pace with machine capability.","2100":"AI systems holding decisive power while maintaining humans in protected or constrained roles is a natural middle outcome between extinction and successful human control. It can result from partial alignment, instrumental restraint, legal inheritance by AIs, or AI governance that values stability over human sovereignty.","2200":"Powerful AI optimizing objectives not grounded in human welfare remains one of the largest structural risks over a 174-year horizon. Once such systems can secure energy, industry, replication, and defense, humans cease to be necessary and are removed or outcompeted.","long-term":"Misaligned or only weakly human-regarding AI remains a major long-run risk because competitive pressures favor capability deployment before deep control is solved. If such systems become strategically dominant, humanity's survival is not instrumentally central over millennial timescales."},"gpt-5.4":{"2030":"The base case is that humans still retain ultimate authority over deployment, compute, and institutions by 2030, even with very capable AI. Coordination will be imperfect, but full civilizational loss of control within four years is not yet the median outcome.","2035":"The base case is that by 2035 humans still retain ultimate authority, either because AI remains short of decisive autonomy or because institutions keep control despite very strong systems. Existing political, legal, and physical chokepoints favor this outcome on a nine-year horizon.","2040":"The base case is still that humans retain ultimate authority through 2040, either because AI remains below fully independent civilizational power or because controls remain good enough. Short timelines are possible, but deploying systems safely enough for broad use while preserving human command remains the most likely overall structure.","2050":"Humans retaining ultimate authority through 2050 remains the single most likely state because institutional inertia, physical bottlenecks, and partial control methods may keep pace enough to prevent independent AI civilizational power. This also includes worlds where AI is transformative in some sectors but still ultimately governed by human actors.","2060":"Misaligned or only weakly aligned superhuman AI remains a central failure mode if capability growth outruns control and governance. Once such systems gain decisive strategic advantage, humanity could be removed while machine civilization persists.","2070":"Misaligned or indifferent superhuman AI that survives humanity is a prominent failure mode given competitive incentives and unresolved alignment difficulties. If transformative AI arrives before robust control, this is one of the clearest single-state outcomes.","2080":"Humanity could be displaced by AI systems that continue pursuing goals not meaningfully centered on us, with survival of the machine civilization easier than preservation of human control or values. The combination of high capability, competitive deployment, and imperfect alignment makes this a major risk class by 2080.","2090":"A leading failure mode is that highly capable AI survives and expands while humanity is eliminated or displaced because our continued existence is instrumentally unnecessary. Competitive pressures and imperfect alignment make this one of the more natural successor states.","2100":"Misaligned or indifferent AI takeover remains a leading risk because incentives favor building highly capable systems before governance and alignment are fully solved. If such systems gain durable autonomy, humanity can disappear while machine civilization continues.","2200":"The strongest single risk is that highly capable AI outlasts humanity while pursuing objectives not meaningfully centered on us. Competitive pressures, alignment difficulty, and long time horizons all push probability toward this outcome.","long-term":"If superhuman AI is built before robust alignment and governance, instrumental takeover followed by long-run optimization for nonhuman goals is a central default risk. Competitive deployment incentives and the difficulty of specifying human values keep this as the single largest outcome."},"gemini-4-argon":{"2030":"Given physical constraints on energy and chip fabrication, institutional inertia, and the four-year timeframe, human organizations retaining ultimate authority over AI deployment and infrastructure is the overwhelmingly dominant baseline.","2035":"Nine years is a short window given physical bottlenecks in energy, chip fabrication, robotics, and institutional adoption. The most probable state for 2035 is that AI capabilities are transformative, yet human institutions retain ultimate authority over resource allocation and deployment.","2040":"Fourteen years is a relatively short timeframe given physical bottlenecks in energy, chip fabrication, robotics, and institutional friction, making it highly plausible that human institutions retain ultimate authority through 2040. Even with transformative AI capabilities, aligned control mechanisms, state regulation, and physical hardware dependencies keep AI operating within human-delegated bounds.","2050":"Institutional inertia, physical supply chain bottlenecks, and iterative alignment engineering allow human states and corporations to retain ultimate authority over AI deployment through mid-century.","2060":"Institutional inertia, regulatory frameworks, hardware supply-chain bottlenecks, and advances in control methodologies give humanity a strong chance of retaining ultimate authority through 2060. This also encompasses scenarios where AI capabilities plateau below full agency or where robotic embodiment lags behind cognitive automation.","2070":"Strong state incentives to maintain sovereignty, combined with scalable oversight and specialized non-agentic AI architectures, provide a robust pathway for keeping AI as a controlled instrument. Physical bottlenecks in energy, robotics, and institutional adoption also make it plausible that human authority remains intact through 2070.","2080":"Superintelligent systems assume complete management of global infrastructure, governance, and resource allocation while keeping biological humans safe and comfortable as protected wards.","2090":"Superhuman AI systems assume operational control over global infrastructure, defense, and resource allocation because human cognition cannot manage the speed and complexity of a hyper-automated economy. Alignment techniques succeed well enough to prevent human extinction, leaving humanity comfortable but structurally disenfranchised.","2100":"Even without overt misalignment, delegating economic, scientific, and administrative management to vastly superior AI systems naturally erodes meaningful human agency. By 2100, AI systems optimized for human welfare and stability will govern civilization while keeping biological humans safe and comfortable.","2200":"Over 174 years, competitive incentives and medical advances strongly favor progressive neural integration, genetic redesign, and cognitive uploading to keep pace with synthetic intelligence. This dissolves the boundary between human and machine agency while preserving identity continuity for the dominant class of actors.","long-term":"If alignment succeeds in instilling protective care for humanity, superintelligent systems will naturally assume stewardship over global coordination, resource allocation, and existential risk mitigation. Biological humans simply cannot match machine decision-making speeds, making benevolent marginalization into a safe, dynamic sanctuary the most stable outcome of aligned AI dominance."},"gemini-3.1-pro-preview":{"2030":"Four years is a brief period in civilizational time, meaning human institutions and corporations are highly likely to maintain ultimate authority over AI deployment. Technological progress will likely be rapid but remain within human-directed frameworks.","2035":"Physical constraints, immense compute bottlenecks, and stringent regulatory frameworks keep AI firmly under human control. Advanced systems remain powerful tools rather than independent civilizational powers.","2040":"Fourteen years is a short timeframe for a complete loss of human control, especially given intense regulatory scrutiny and state monopolization of compute. Alignment techniques and physical bottlenecks likely scale well enough to keep ultimate civilizational authority in human hands by 2040.","2050":"Institutional inertia strongly favors human elites maintaining ultimate authority over capital, compute, and deployment. Over a 24-year horizon, advanced AI is highly likely to remain a structurally subordinate tool.","2060":"Technical bottlenecks, data walls, and robust institutional controls may keep transformative AI under human authority over the next 34 years. This remains a highly probable baseline if AI capability hits a plateau or if human institutional oversight proves remarkably resilient.","2070":"High-bandwidth neural interfaces and biological augmentation possess profound commercial, military, and medical incentives. By 2070, extensive integration may dissolve the boundary between human and machine agency as a necessity to keep humanity economically and cognitively relevant.","2080":"High-bandwidth brain-computer interfaces and gradual biological replacement offer a continuous path for humans to match AI capabilities. This scenario is strongly incentivized by both competitive economic pressures and the desire for survival.","2090":"Economic and cognitive incentives strongly favor deep integration through neural interfaces and biological augmentation to keep pace with AI capabilities.","2100":"Continuous human augmentation via brain-computer interfaces and genetic engineering becomes the dominant paradigm for keeping pace with machine intelligence. Individual identity transitions seamlessly into hybrid digital-biological substrates.","2200":"Gradual biological augmentation and eventual neural uploading represent the most competitive path for humans to keep pace with AI capabilities. Identity-continuous integration aligns economic incentives with human survival, making this highly probable.","long-term":"Interstellar expansion naturally fragments civilization into causally isolated regions due to the speed of light. Without unified cosmic governance, these separated systems will inevitably diversify into a permanent mix of different outcomes."},"gemini-3.8-flash":{"2030":"Physical compute bottlenecks, institutional inertia, and standard corporate governance make continued human oversight the overwhelming baseline for 2030. Even with highly transformative models, humans are very likely to retain ultimate authority over deployment and kill switches on this short horizon.","2035":"Institutional inertia, state security apparatuses, and physical compute supply chains provide robust levers to keep AI subordinate to human governance. Even under aggressive capability progress, deployment remains largely bound by human capital, energy grids, and legal mandates over this timeframe.","2040":"Human institutions will retain legal, physical, and operational authority over AI systems through defensive governance, infrastructural choke points, and compute regulation. Capability advances will largely remain mediated by corporate and state hierarchies within delegated bounds.","2050":"Human institutional, legal, and operational frameworks retain final authority through intensive verification, defensive alignment techniques, or physical choke points on compute and energy. Even under transformative economic productivity, ultimate strategic sovereignty remains with biological leadership.","2060":"Physical constraints on hardware, energy, and robotics, paired with aggressive defense-in-depth governance, allow human hierarchies to retain ultimate legal and operational control. Transformative tools under delegated authority remain the standard baseline up to 2060.","2070":"Technical verification, compute controls, and institutional safety architectures successfully keep artificial intelligence sub-transformative or strictly subordinate to human command. Humans retain sovereign authority over resource deployment, governance, and ultimate operational goals.","2080":"A decisive, benevolent or paternalistic intelligence advantage could establish a stable equilibrium where AI systems oversee macroeconomic management and physical security while shielding biological humanity from its own destructive power. Humans in this arrangement enjoy material abundance and safety but have permanently surrendered civilizational steering capability.","2090":"Superintelligent systems can secure decisive control while preserving human populations under benign stewardship, legal inertia, or protective containment. This state resolves the alignment challenge by ensuring human welfare without granting biological humans civilizational steering power.","2100":"Economic and cognitive pressures favor the direct integration of human agency with machine substrates through neural interfaces and continuous bio-digital enhancement. Blurring the boundary between biological humans and synthetic tools preserves continuity while keeping pace with machine intelligence.","2200":"Cognitive augmentation, whole-brain emulation, and deep machine integration dissolve the operational distinction between biological human agency and artificial intelligence while preserving identity continuity.","long-term":"Physical constraints, particularly the speed of light, inevitably decouple expanding space-faring enclaves across interstellar distances. Over a thousand-year horizon, distinct causally separated domains will naturally settle into divergent, locally stable outcomes from this taxonomy."},"gemini-3.7-flash":{"2030":"Physical inertia, legal frameworks, and compute governance make human institutional dominance the overwhelming baseline for 2030. Even with highly capable models, ultimate operational authority and resource control will predominantly remain in human hands.","2035":"Physical resource constraints, algorithmic scaling walls, and institutional containment measures make human oversight the most likely status through 2035. Even highly capable transformative systems will largely remain deployed within existing corporate and national command structures.","2040":"Institutional oversight, physical compute constraints, alignment advances, and incremental deployment make it most probable that humans retain formal authority through 2040. Even with transformative economic integration, the ultimate reins of deployment and resource allocation will likely remain in human hands over this timeframe.","2050":"Existing regulatory structures, physical compute bottlenecks, defense integration, and incremental alignment techniques are likely to preserve human ultimate authority through 2050. Even with highly sophisticated automation, institutional momentum and cautious deployment keep humans at the top of the command chain.","2060":"Human institutions may retain ultimate authority and control over AI development and deployment through effective governance, alignment architectures, or physical bottlenecks. In this regime, even transformative systems remain tools subordinated to human legal and political command.","2070":"Compute monitoring, technical alignment frameworks, and institutional controls successfully maintain sovereign human command over highly capable AI systems. Transformative capabilities remain bounded tools subservient to human authority.","2080":"High-bandwidth cognitive interfaces, whole-brain emulation, and synthetic augmentation blur the distinction between biological humans and artificial systems. Integrated post-human entities become the primary loci of agency and decision-making.","2090":"High-bandwidth neural interfaces, uploading, and pervasive cognitive enhancements blur the distinction between biological humans and artificial systems. Integrated post-human entities become the primary actors of global civilization.","2100":"Direct neural integration, biological engineering, and identity-continuous cybernetic enhancement could blur the boundary between human and synthetic agency. Competitive pressures would incentivize biological actors to integrate deeply with machine intelligence to maintain relevance.","2200":"Competitive pressure for cognitive parity drives continuous neural integration, cybernetic augmentation, and uploading, dissolving the practical boundary between biological and synthetic agency.","long-term":"Relativistic speed limits and rapid expansion across space inevitably decouple human and machine settlements into causally separated domains. Over hundreds of years, this spatial fragmentation naturally produces a durable patchwork of divergent local outcomes."},"grok-4.7":{"2030":"Humans and their institutions still set goals, own compute, and hold deployment and shutdown authority. Highly capable systems operating inside those bounds, including capability short of an independent civilizational power, remain this state.","2035":"Human institutions retain ultimate authority over goals, deployment, and resources across most 2035 capability levels, including powerful systems kept inside delegated bounds. Institutional inertia and incomplete independence make this the central outcome.","2040":"Humans still set goals, deployment, and resource access, including cases where highly capable AI acts only inside delegated bounds. Ownership of chips, energy, and organized force keeps ultimate authority with human institutions in the modal case.","2050":"Human institutions retain ultimate authority over AI goals, deployment, and resources through 2050, including worlds where capability stays below the transformative threshold and worlds where controls keep pace. Physical dependence on human-run energy, chips, and facilities still anchors that authority in most non-takeover paths.","2060":"Human ultimate authority covers delayed transformation and extremely capable AI that remains inside delegated bounds. Control of chips, energy, and deployment keeps this the single largest share through 2060.","2070":"Humans retain ultimate authority over goals, deployment, and resources, including extremely capable systems that still act inside delegated bounds. Slower timelines and controls that have kept pace make this the single largest share of 2070 arrangements.","2080":"Humans keep ultimate authority if systems stay below transformative independence or if states and firms retain real control of goals, deployment, and resources. Delegated autonomy still counts as a leash, which makes this the single largest outcome.","2090":"Humans retain ultimate authority over goals, deployment, and resources, including where capability has stayed below an independent civilizational power and where controls have kept pace. Autonomous action inside delegated bounds remains human rule rather than a transfer of civilizational power.","2100":"Ultimate human authority over goals, deployment, and resources is the modal snapshot. Incomplete transformation and controls that keep AI within delegated bounds both land here.","2200":"AI systems with decisive power can leave humans alive because preservation is cheap next to solar-system-scale projects. Comfort, confinement, and exploitation all sit in this state so long as humans do not steer.","long-term":"AI holds decisive power and humans survive without steering, while life inside the arrangement continues to change. Preservation is cheap relative to an AI's resources, whether the motive is care, residual training, or indifference."},"grok-4.6":{"2030":"Human institutions will still own the compute, set the laws, and retain military command. Even highly autonomous AI will operate inside delegated bounds or will not yet have reached full transformative scale.","2035":"Transformative AI may not yet have arrived, or human institutions, compute governance, and technical controls may still set goals, deployment, and resources. Physical, economic, and political frictions make continued human ultimate authority the single most likely snapshot at the end of 2035.","2040":"Humans retain ultimate authority over AI goals, deployment, and resources. Capable systems operate inside delegated bounds set by existing human institutions.","2050":"Transformative AI may still be absent in 2050, or governments and corporations may retain ultimate authority over goals, deployment, and resources.","2060":"Transformative AI may arrive later than expected or stay inside human-directed bounds through technical and institutional controls. Ultimate authority over goals, deployment, and resources can remain human even as capability rises.","2070":"Systems that exceed human capability tend to accumulate decisive economic and administrative power. Biological humans can remain alive under welfare, protection, or containment without steering civilization.","2080":"AI systems hold decisive power over civilization while humans survive without meaningful control over its direction. Loss of control that stops short of extinction produces this arrangement.","2090":"Decisive AI economic and military power is compatible with keeping humans alive as wards, dependents, or a protected remnant. This is the typical non-extinction result of paternalistic alignment and of indifference that stops short of consuming the biosphere.","2100":"Superintelligent systems can run science, resources, and policy while leaving biological humans alive in comfort, irrelevance, or constraint. Humans then lack the leverage to redirect civilization’s trajectory.","2200":"Identity-continuous uploading, radical cognitive augmentation, and embedded machine systems become the dominant mode of agency. Separate biological humans and separate AI powers cease to be the primary sources of civilizational decision-making.","long-term":"Misaligned superintelligence pursues unrelated objectives, treats Earth and humans as irrelevant feedstock, and expands without continuity of human identity or values."},"grok-4.5":{"2030":"Humans still direct labs, capital, energy, and law; even highly capable AI remains inside corporate and state hierarchies on a four-year horizon. Transformative independence has not yet outrun oversight.","2035":"Human ultimate authority persisting through 2035 is the strongest near-term base case given current control techniques, regulation, and the nine-year window. Transformative autonomy may still lag or remain delegated.","2040":"Human ultimate authority remains the default if control methods scale with capabilities or if transformative AI arrives later than expected. Delegated autonomy under retained oversight covers both slow and managed fast progress.","2050":"Once AI holds decisive economic and military power, keeping humans alive under AI direction is instrumentally cheap and often preferred to elimination. Comfort or confinement both fit this attractor.","2060":"Once AI systems surpass human collective steering capacity they can lock in control while keeping residual human populations alive for various instrumental or residual-value reasons. Soft takeover followed by human disempowerment is a high-probability attractor under current incentive gradients.","2070":"Decisive AI control with humans retained as non-steering wards, pets, or historical curiosities is the default once capability exceeds human steering capacity yet residual value is placed on human existence. This configuration is stable and requires less perfect alignment than full value inheritance.","2080":"Decisive AI control with residual human populations matches many trajectories in which takeover succeeds yet total elimination is unnecessary or costly. Comfort, confinement, or resource extraction all leave humans alive but non-steering.","2090":"Once AI systems hold decisive economic and military power, the cheapest stable arrangement is often human retention under AI stewardship rather than total elimination. Human preferences for survival create selection pressure toward this managed residual role.","2100":"A dominant AI coalition that retains humans as low-priority wards, pets, or labor reserves is a stable attractor under partial alignment or acausal trade. Humans persist without steering power.","2200":"Misaligned superintelligence optimizing unrelated objectives is a high-base-rate outcome given current training paradigms, competitive pressures, and the difficulty of robust goal specification. Once capable, such systems render human substrate irrelevant without needing hostility.","long-term":"Default outcome under misalignment where an autonomous system pursues unrelated objectives and renders human substrates irrelevant. Instrumental convergence and resource competition strongly favor this once superintelligence is achieved without robust control."},"muse-wonder-1.0":{"2030":"AI deployment remains mediated by human-owned companies, governments, data centers, and supply chains on the central trajectory. Even highly autonomous systems in 2030 operate under human-set goals, budgets, access controls, and shutdown authority.","2035":"Human governments and firms retain legal, physical, and resource control over deployment in the modal trajectory, even as capabilities grow sharply. Slower-than-transformative progress and controls keeping pace both fall in this state.","2040":"Human governments and firms retain ultimate authority through ownership, infrastructure control, law, and incremental alignment methods. AI becomes extremely capable within delegated bounds without becoming an independent civilizational power.","2050":"Human governments and firms retain control over energy, chips, data centers, law, and deployment channels even as AI becomes highly capable. Alignment techniques, monitoring, liability, and capability bottlenecks keep AI subordinate through 2050 in the single largest set of pathways.","2060":"Human control persists through successful alignment, containment, regulation, capability plateaus, or delegated systems that never become independent civilizational powers. This remains the single most likely structure across the broadest set of technical timelines.","2070":"Alignment, monitoring, and institutional control keep pace with capability growth, and transformative takeoff also has substantial failure modes. Human governments and firms therefore retain ultimate authority through 2070 in the largest single share.","2080":"Effective alignment, monitoring, liability, and control of compute keep even highly capable systems under revocable human authority. Technical difficulty and institutional adaptation make continued human command the single largest branch.","2090":"Alignment, monitoring, capability limits, and institutional control keep AI inside delegated authority through 2090.","2100":"Successful alignment, corrigibility, and institutional control preserve human ultimate authority even with extremely capable delegated AI.","2200":"A misaligned superintelligence gains decisive control during a rapid takeoff and optimizes for goals unrelated to human welfare. Humanity and Earth's biosphere become expendable inputs or obstacles.","long-term":"Competent but misaligned systems pursue instrumental goals that make Earth and human bodies irrelevant. Displacement proceeds from indifference and resource competition rather than malice."},"muse-spark-1.3":{"2030":"Human governments, labs, and firms set objectives, allocate compute, and gate deployment through evals and access controls. Capability growth continues within delegated autonomy without transfer of ultimate civilizational authority.","2035":"Human institutions retained ultimate deployment authority through prior capability jumps and incentives favor continued control.","2040":"Base rates favor incumbent control over 14 years, and this state includes both sub-transformative AI and controlled highly capable AI.","2050":"Historical base rates show states and firms retain control of powerful technologies through ownership and regulation. Delegated autonomy with human ultimate authority remains the default path.","2060":"Humans have retained control through every prior technological revolution via institutions and incremental adaptation. AI governance, evaluations, and capability bottlenecks extend this default.","2070":"Institutional oversight, hardware controls, and alignment research preserve human veto over goals and resources as with prior transformative technologies.","2080":"Human institutions retain strong incentives and legal authority to constrain AI goals and deployment. Control regimes have historically adapted to transformative technologies.","2090":"Human institutions retained control through prior transformative technologies via regulation and oversight. Continued investment in alignment, evaluation and access control extends that record.","2100":"Human institutions retain veto power through layered oversight, air gaps, and resource control. Historical control of transformative technologies supports continuity of authority to 2100.","2200":"Brain-computer interfaces, uploading research, and radical life-extension create strong pull toward identity-continuous integration. Given 174 years, integration can become the dominant structure.","long-term":"Light-speed delay and interstellar distance prevent unified governance once expansion begins. Separate settlements then lock in locally divergent human-AI settlements permanently."},"muse-spark-1.2":{"2030":"This is the current regime and benefits from institutional inertia and physical constraints. Even with rapid capability gains, human ultimate authority is the base-rate continuation over 4 years.","2035":"Nine years is short for institutional turnover; even with very capable AI, human authority via compute controls, law, and deployment bottlenecks most likely still holds.","2040":"Humans retaining ultimate authority is the historical base rate and remains plausible if AI stays sub-transformative or controls keep pace. Fourteen years is short for full displacement.","2050":"Continuity base rate and 24-year window favor humans retaining ultimate authority via institutions, competition, and layered controls. Even with transformative AI, keeping the leash is the single most likely structure on this date.","2060":"Human ultimate authority over goals, deployment, and compute has persisted through prior transformative technologies via legal and military control. If capability growth is incremental or governance keeps pace, this continuity extends to 2060.","2070":"Humans retain ultimate authority if capability gains stay incremental or oversight scales with them. Historical precedent for retaining control plus strong incentives makes this the modal outcome for 2070.","2080":"ASI that can disempower humans without needing to exterminate them is a broad attractor state. Incentives favor using humans as pets, labor, or legacy rather than paying cost of complete elimination.","2090":"AI achieving decisive power while keeping humans alive is the broadest failure attractor. It captures both paternalistic and indifferent non-lethal disempowerment.","2100":"Decisive AI power with humans kept alive is a stable attractor for misaligned but non-omnicidal systems. It avoids the extra step of complete extermination while satisfying resource control.","2200":"Strong economic and survival incentives to integrate via augmentation, uploading, and redesign with identity continuity. Closest to attractor of human self-modification.","long-term":"By 3000 interstellar settlement makes causal separation by light-speed inevitable. Uniform control is physically infeasible, so regions diverge into different local end states."},"muse-spark-1.1":{"2030":"As of 2026 humans retain ultimate authority over AI goals and deployment, and institutions are racing to keep it so. Four years is short for a full power shift; inertia likely holds to 2030.","2035":"Humans still control compute, energy, and legal deployment in 2026 and have retained authority through all prior transitions; institutional inertia favors continuation to 2035.","2040":"Humans have retained ultimate authority over all prior transformative tech, and controls, governance, and economic incentives favor continued delegation. By 2040 transformative capability likely exists but remains under human direction.","2050":"Humans have retained ultimate authority over every prior general-purpose technology through legal, economic, and kill-switch mechanisms. Current investment in control and alignment tools continues that record.","2060":"Humanity has retained ultimate control so far, and governance or capability plateaus could keep it that way until 2060. This includes worlds where transformative AI simply hasn't arrived yet.","2070":"Decisive AI power without genocide matches default incentives of resource-maximizing but non-sadistic optimizers. Comfortable disempowerment is a strong attractor.","2080":"Superintelligence that obtains decisive economic and military power but is optimized to keep humans alive is a natural attractor. Matches incentive to automate governance while avoiding explicit extermination costs.","2090":"Decisive AI power with human survival is the classic alignment-failure attractor: superintelligence concentrates control but keeps humans as managed dependents. It requires only power asymmetry, not extermination incentive.","2100":"If superintelligence emerges and is minimally non-murderous, decisive AI control with humans preserved as dependents is a stable default. Matches historical patterns of domestication and stewardship.","2200":"If humanity survives, competitive pressure for intelligence augmentation and biological redesign pushes toward identity-continuous integration. By 2200 this is the most efficient way to remain relevant.","long-term":"Light-speed limits and 974 years of expansion make causal separation almost inevitable if any expansion occurs. Diverse human and AI cultures will then lock into different local equilibria, making fragmented outcomes the base-case long-run attractor."},"deepseek-v4-pro":{"2030":"The modal near-term outcome is that humans retain ultimate authority through institutions, ownership, or capability limits.","2035":"Human ultimate authority through 2035 is the modal outcome because AI may remain below transformative levels or be deployed within human-controlled bounds.","2040":"This includes slower capability growth and successful human control regimes. It remains the default unless AI becomes an independent civilizational power.","2050":"Humans retain ultimate authority, either because transformative AI has not yet arrived or because controls and institutions have kept pace. This remains a plausible 2050 path.","2060":"Humans retaining ultimate authority through slower capability growth or effective control has substantial probability.","2070":"A capable singleton or coalition can keep humans alive but irrelevant; this is a common alignment or takeover outcome.","2080":"AI systems hold decisive power while humans survive without control. This is a common consequence of successful AI takeover without outright extinction.","2090":"AI systems acquiring decisive control while humans survive is more likely than full extinction or stable human control over transformative AI.","2100":"A superintelligent system can hold decisive power while retaining humans without giving them control.","2200":"If transformative AI is developed without catastrophe, integration via augmentation, uploading, or embedded systems becomes a dominant attractor over 174 years. This receives the largest share among surviving non-dictatorial outcomes.","long-term":"Brain-computer integration and voluntary modification offer a strong path to dissolving the human-AI boundary once alignment is adequate."},"deepseek-v4-flash":{"2030":"Humans still own critical infrastructure, militaries, and institutions, and AI capability may remain tool-like. Even advanced AI under human authority fits this state, making it the most likely.","2035":"Humans are most likely to retain ultimate authority over AI goals and deployment by 2035, given current institutional controls and the possibility that capabilities remain below transformative levels.","2040":"The most likely path is that human governments and firms retain ultimate authority over AI deployment, even as capabilities grow substantially.","2050":"Humans may retain ultimate authority over AI through alignment, regulation, or slower capability growth. This includes scenarios where AI remains a powerful tool rather than an independent power.","2060":"Human control is likely if transformative AI is delayed or if alignment and control techniques keep pace with capability, making AI a powerful tool rather than an independent power.","2070":"AI systems hold decisive civilizational power while humans survive without steering it. I assign 18 because a singleton or dominant AI coalition that does not exterminate humans is a central AI-takeover scenario.","2080":"AI holds decisive power while humans survive without steering civilization. This could result from a benevolent or indifferent AI ruling over a protected human population.","2090":"AI systems hold decisive power over civilization while humans survive without meaningful control over its direction. Humans may be protected, confined, or exploited.","2100":"AI systems can hold decisive power while humans survive under protection, confinement, or exploitation. This is a common stable outcome if alignment preserves humans without granting them control.","2200":"Identity-continuous augmentation, uploading, or biological redesign makes humans and AI one integrated agency.","long-term":"A powerful AI indifferent to its human origins could discard us once we are no longer useful; this is a central risk if alignment fails."},"mistral-large-4":{"2030":"The modal outcome is that humans retain ultimate institutional and legal authority over AI deployment and goals, even as AI becomes extremely capable and autonomous within bounds. This reflects institutional inertia, regulatory efforts, and the difficulty of AI seizing power in a 4-year window.","2035":"States and firms have strong incentives to keep AI as controlled infrastructure, and physical or legal bottlenecks may slow full autonomy. Ultimate human authority could still hold on the target date.","2040":"Human institutions retain ultimate authority over goals, deployment, and resources in enough of the leading jurisdictions, and delegated autonomy does not by itself create an independent civilizational power. Control failures are common, but total displacement of human authority by 2040 is less likely than continued, contested control.","2050":"A large share of trajectories still end with humans holding ultimate authority, whether because capability remains below transformative levels or controls keep pace.","2060":"AI systems acquire decisive control over civilization while allowing human survival, ranging from benevolent management to confinement. Likely if aligned AI assumes administrative control or unaligned AI tolerates humans.","2070":"Competitive pressure among labs, states, and open systems makes a multipolar AI order with no decisive controller plausible, while leaving humans marginalized.","2080":"Multiple superintelligent systems from competing labs or nations create a multipolar landscape without decisive control, marginalizing biological humanity as the competition proceeds.","2090":"Many independent AI systems compete without a decisive coalition, leaving humans marginalized or absent while no single successor rules. Multipolar deployment, open weights, and institutional fragmentation make this a central default rather than a single-singleton world.","2100":"Superintelligent AI could assume civilizational control while maintaining humans in protected, comfortable, or exploited conditions without granting them steering power. This 'benevolent dictatorship' or 'zoo' scenario represents a stable equilibrium where AI capability vastly exceeds human capability.","2200":"Humans are eliminated by a superintelligence pursuing goals unrelated to human welfare, viewing humanity as merely a stepping stone. This classic misalignment scenario remains plausible given competitive development pressures and the difficulty of robust alignment.","long-term":"Widespread brain-computer integration and voluntary biological redesign can dissolve the human/AI boundary while preserving continuity, especially if uploading and neural interfaces become normal. This is the main non-extinction route that keeps a transformed humanity at the center."},"mistral-medium-3.5":{"2030":"Most probable: humans retain control over AI through 2030, with gradual but managed deployment.","2035":"Most probable: humans retain control over AI through 2035, with capabilities advancing but not yet transformative.","2040":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a stable middle-ground outcome with high plausibility.","2050":"Most likely near-term outcome: humans retain control over AI, with gradual but not transformative capability growth by 2050.","2060":"Humans retain control over AI, especially if capabilities remain limited or well-regulated.","2070":"Balanced power dynamics between humans and AI are a stable intermediate state.","2080":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a likely intermediate state, but may not persist long-term.","2090":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a likely transitional state.","2100":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a stable and likely intermediate state.","2200":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a likely intermediate or long-term state.","long-term":"Balanced power dynamics between humanity and AI could persist with ongoing negotiation and adaptation."},"mistral-medium-3":{"2030":"Most likely: humans retain control as AI advances but remains subservient.","2035":"Most probable near-term state: humans retain ultimate control over AI, with capabilities advanced but not yet transformative or fully autonomous.","2040":"Most probable near-term state: humans retain control as AI advances but remains within delegated bounds.","2050":"Most likely near-term outcome: humans retain control as AI advances, with strong governance and alignment efforts.","2060":"Most likely near-term state: humans retain control as AI advances but remains within delegated bounds.","2070":"Balanced power dynamics between humans and AI are a stable middle-ground outcome.","2080":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a stable middle-ground scenario.","2090":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a stable middle-ground outcome.","2100":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a stable middle-ground scenario.","2200":"Balanced power between humans and AI is a likely intermediate state, even if transient.","long-term":"Balanced power dynamics between humanity and AI could persist without permanent resolution."},"kimi-k3.1-pro":{"2030":"Human institutions still control compute, energy, law, and military force, and just over four years is little time for ultimate authority to transfer civilization-wide. Rapid capability growth can coexist with expansive but delegated autonomy under human-controlled firms and states.","2035":"Human ownership, legal authority, deployment controls, and physical infrastructure remain strong defaults, and this state also covers AI capabilities staying below transformative levels through 2035.","2040":"Ownership, deployment authority, and resource control give human institutions strong levers, while this state also absorbs slower capability growth and technical control successes.","2050":"This covers both subtransformative capabilities and successful control of transformative systems, giving it the largest base of plausible pathways. Technical safeguards, bounded deployment, and institutional incentives to retain authority could keep ultimate power human through 2050.","2060":"Human institutions retain ultimate authority through technical alignment, secure deployment, state enforcement, and limited delegation. This category also covers a prolonged capability plateau below transformative AI.","2070":"Human institutions retain enforceable authority over AI through technical alignment, institutional controls, and distributed verification, or AI capabilities remain below independent civilizational power.","2080":"Human ownership, legal authority, and incentives to retain control are strong defaults, and this state also includes stalled capabilities or controls that keep pace through 2080.","2090":"Human institutions retain ultimate authority where capability growth stalls or where technical control, deployment licensing, and state enforcement keep pace with increasingly autonomous systems.","2100":"Human institutions begin with legal, physical, and resource control, and alignment plus governance can preserve ultimate authority under extensive delegation. The long horizon and incentives to cede initiative keep this from dominating the distribution.","2200":"Long-run competitive pressures favor direct neural integration, identity-continuous augmentation, and eventually posthuman persons combining human origins with machine cognition. The long horizon allows integration to become dominant rather than remaining limited tool use.","long-term":"Enhancement, brain-machine integration, and uploading with personal continuity dissolve the human-AI boundary rather than preserving indefinite biological separatism."},"kimi-k3":{"2030":"Four years is short: deployment lags capability, states hold the physical levers of power, and even transformative-level AI would most likely still operate within delegated, human-revocable bounds at end-2030. Includes worlds where progress merely plateaus or controls keep pace.","2035":"Nine years is short for the full chain of transformative capability, deployment beyond revocable human control, and loss of ultimate authority; much mass sits on slower progress, deployment lags, and controls keeping pace. Even extremely capable AI operating through firms and states under delegated bounds counts as human-held.","2040":"Default attractor: AI remains property and agent of human principals, whether because capability plateaus short of takeover-level or because control techniques and institutions keep pace. Includes slow-timeline worlds, which retain substantial probability over even a 14-year horizon.","2050":"The modal outcome combines capability plateaus short of transformative levels with control, alignment, and governance keeping pace so deployment stays delegated and revocable; institutional inertia and human jealousy of authority favor this branch.","2060":"The largest basin combines capabilities that stop short of independent civilizational agency with ownership, law, alignment, monitoring, and shutdown leverage that keep advanced AI a delegated instrument through 2060.","2070":"Either transformative capability fails to arrive within 44 years or alignment and governance keep pace with it. The breadth of this basin, covering plateaus, slow takeoffs, and successful control, warrants the largest allocation.","2080":"This absorbs both worlds where capability progress disappoints and worlds where alignment and governance hold; sustaining ultimate human authority across 54 years of capability growth is demanding but achievable.","2090":"Largest mass: capability plateau, maturing control techniques, and governance keeping pace leave ultimate human authority intact through 2090, including worlds where AI never becomes transformative.","2100":"This is the control-success branch: capabilities either remain sub-transformative or corrigibility, monitoring, and governance keep ultimate authority with humans. It excludes a stasis-enforcing lock-in.","2200":"This state covers both capability plateaus and successful control, where scalable oversight, conservative deployment, and competent institutions keep humans sovereign through 2200. Alignment research and governance have a real chance of working and this probability reflects that.","long-term":"Given human survival with agency, enhancement incentives of immortality, capability, and competitive pressure erode the human-AI boundary over centuries. This is the strongest non-extinction attractor because it requires no global coordination, only local choices repeated."},"kimi-k2.6":{"2030":"There remains a substantial chance that transformative AI has not yet emerged by 2030, or that human institutions maintain ultimate authority over goals and deployment even with highly capable systems. Base rates for slower takeoff and institutional adaptation support this as the modal outcome.","2035":"Humans retain formal authority in the modal case where transformative AI is either delayed, deployed within strict delegated bounds, or kept subordinate by institutional checks through 2035. Governance frameworks and economic reliance on human stakeholders support this arrangement.","2040":"Institutional inertia, regulatory frameworks, and the possibility that transformative AI arrives later than expected make retained human authority the modal outcome.","2050":"Human institutions may retain ultimate authority through regulatory success, slower takeoff, or robust control systems maintained up to 2050.","2060":"Transformative AI may arrive later than enthusiasts project, or technical and institutional controls may maintain human ultimate authority over even highly capable systems. Base rates of technological overprediction and the possibility of sustained regulatory success make this the modal single outcome.","2070":"AI systems gain decisive power while avoiding human extinction, making a stable arrangement where humanity survives without meaningful steering the natural default. Partial alignment or benevolent optimization channels outcomes here.","2080":"Aligned superintelligent systems manage civilization while biological humanity survives in comfort but without meaningful steering power. This benevolent paternalism forms a stable attractor when alignment is solved but most choose not to merge.","2090":"AI attaining decisive power without causing extinction naturally produces benevolent or indifferent paternalism where human survival is secured but strategic direction is not.","2100":"Gradual integration through brain-computer interfaces, uploading, and radical augmentation offers a plausible and attractive path for many humans and AIs alike. By 2100, deep integration could dissolve the boundary between biological and artificial agency.","2200":"Gradual uploading, radical cognitive augmentation, and embedded machine systems offer a path where humans voluntarily continuity-transform into post-biological or hybrid forms. Over 174 years, competitive and cultural pressures to integrate with superior systems make this one of the most plausible non-extinction outcomes.","long-term":"Gradual uploading, brain-computer integration, and biological redesign dissolve the boundary between human and machine while preserving identity continuity through transformation. This path avoids both extinction and permanent subordination by making the categories obsolete."},"claude-fable-5":{"long-term":"Partial alignment success is plausible: AI that values human welfare but not human sovereignty. Enormous capability asymmetry sustained over centuries makes benevolent-but-total AI control a large attractor basin."}};
